.Dd $Mdocdate: August 31 2026 $
.Dt GETEXECPATH 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getexecpath
.Nd attempt to get executable path
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn getexecpath "char *buf" "size_t bufsize"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn getexecpath
function retrieves the absolute pathname of this executable.
If the pathname is shorter than
.Fa bufsize
bytes, it is copied into the memory referenced by
.Fa buf .
The recommended buffer size is
.Dv PATH_MAX .
This pathname has been canonicalized in the same manner as
.Xr realpath 3 .
.Pp
The two main reasons why
.Fn getexecpath
can return failure are if the executable was started in a directory
which is no longer linked to the filesystem, or if the absolute pathname
would exceed
.Dv PATH_MAX .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn getexecpath
function will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOENT
.Xr execve 2 could not determine the executable pathname.
.It Bq Er ERANGE
The
.Ar bufsize
argument is too small.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn getexecpath
function first appeared in
.Ox 8.0 .
A variety of system-dependent (and often flawed) solutions for this
problem of retrieving the executable path are available in other operating
systems.
.Fn getexecpath
aspires to do better.
